        433.168701171875,
        518.7904052734375,
        441.9656677246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "executing a ﬂight path consisting of a straightaway and a 90◦turn (see Figure 35). While we",
      "bbox": [
        88.19700622558594,
        443.142578125,
        518.6517333984375,
        453.920654296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "recognize that our analysis makes many assumptions and approximations, we still believe that it",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        457.07867431640625,
        518.7705688476562,
        465.8756408691406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "provides some valuable insights. First, the energy advantage at smaller vehicle scales is signiﬁcantly",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        469.03466796875,
        518.8203125,
        477.8316345214844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "greater than at larger scales. Secondly, ﬁxed-wing UAVs almost always outperform quadrotors,",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        480.9896545410156,
        518.7506103515625,
        489.78662109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "except in very persistent hover conditions (i.e., when a ﬂight path is entirely made up of tight",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        492.94464111328125,
        518.8004760742188,
        501.7416076660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "turns), and often signiﬁcantly outperform quadrotors (by a factor of two or more) at urban scales",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        504.8996276855469,
        518.7706298828125,
        513.6965942382812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(e.g., Edge540XL with 20 m straightaways and 2 m turns). We also observed that accounting for turn",
